% A derivation cycle built from the CLI (D0, aida-cyclic-fragment,
% 2026-09-16): argA argues A from a presumed Q; cQ refutes Q from B
% (an OBLIGATION), which aB supplies by argB from a presumed R; cR
% refutes R from a PRESUMED A.  On the graph
%
%   A[t] <- Q[t]:pres      cQs: Q[c] <- B[t]:obli
%   B[t] <- R[t]:pres      cR:  R[c] <- A[t]:pres
%
% linked through contrariness at Q and at R: a cycle through A, which
% the compiler used to refuse and now classifies ("fragment: cyclic").
%
% Since the asymmetric guard (2026-09-25) the cycle RESOLVES: cQ derives
% Q[c] from the obligation B, B rests on the presumed R, and R is refuted
% by cR from the presumed A.  A bare presumption does not guard back
% against a derivation, so R[c] is IN, R[t] and hence B[t] are OUT, cQ
% fails, Q[t] stands, and argA carries A.  One labelling, two-valued:
%
%   A[t] IN   Q[t] IN   Q[c] OUT   R[c] IN   R[t] OUT   B[t] OUT
%
% `evaluate d credulous` is therefore argA applied to the presumption of
% Q, not the bare presumption of A that cR supplies.  Before the guard
% change every statement but A was UNDEC and there were three preferred
% labellings.  Since Phase B the debate term is unfolded from the
% document graph (before Phase B the composed term buried the site inside
% the attacker and the evaluation was OPEN - the gap that motivated
% graph-first).
lk.
declare A,B,Q,R:bool.
declare qa : (Q->A).
declare rb : (R->B).
declare bnq : (B->~Q).
declare anr : (A->~R).

start argument argA A
cut (Q->A) r.
axiom qa.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom r.
end argument

start argument argB B
cut (R->B) r.
axiom rb.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom r.
end argument

% Refute Q from B, B left as an obligation.
start counterargument cQ Q
cut (B->~Q) th.
axiom bnq.
elim.
next.
elim.
axiom.
end counterargument

% Refute R from A, A taken by default.
start counterargument cR R
cut (A->~R) th.
axiom anr.
elim.
by default.
next.
elim.
axiom.
end counterargument

debate pro open aB : B.
argB.
attack cR argB.
hora est.
debate con open cQs : Q.
cQ.
support argB cQ.
attack cR argB.
hora est.
debate pro open d : A.
argA.
attack cQ argA.
support argB cQ.
attack cR argB.
hora est.

graph d
label d
label d preferred
evaluate d credulous
evaluate d skeptical
